{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>If Elohim will be with me, etc.</b> Far be it to say that Jacob was in doubt about the promise of HaShem, blessed be He, for Elohim had already said to him, ״וְהִנֵּה אָנֹכִי עִמָּךְ וּשְׁמַרְתִּיךָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֵּלֵךְ״ (Genesis 28:15). Rather, the explanation of the matter is that Jacob did not ask at all about the guarding of the body, for he had already been promised that; rather, he now asked about the guarding of the soul from sin. And the proof of this is that in HaShem's promise, blessed be He, it says ״וּשְׁמַרְתִּיךָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֵּלֵךְ״, whereas Jacob said ״וּשְׁמָרַנִי בַּדֶּרֶךְ הַזֶּה אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי הוֹלֵךְ״ — and he should have said ״וּשְׁמָרַנִי בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֵלֵךְ״. Moreover, the word ״אָנֹכִי״ is entirely superfluous. The Holy One, blessed be He, said ״וַהֲשִׁבֹתִיךָ אֶל הָאֲדָמָה״ (Genesis 28:15) — He suspended the return upon HaShem, blessed be He; but Jacob said ״וְשַׁבְתִּי בְשָׁלוֹם אֶל בֵּית אָבִי״ (Genesis 28:21) — he suspended the return upon himself. HaShem, blessed be He, said ״אֶל הָאֲדָמָה״, and Jacob said ״אֶל בֵּית אָבִי״. From all these changes we may learn that Jacob asked only about the guarding of the soul from sin, for this is a matter that depends on a person's free choice and also requires divine assistance, as David prayed, saying (Psalms 119:37) ״הַעֲבֵר עֵינַי מֵרְאוֹת שָׁוְא״. Therefore, regarding the guarding of the body, He said ״וּשְׁמַרְתִּיךָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֵּלֵךְ״ — He used the language of ״כֹּל״ (all), for all roads are presumed dangerous and his going was upon many roads. But the good road is only one; therefore he said ״וּשְׁמָרַנִי בַּדֶּרֶךְ הַזֶּה״, as if pointing with his finger to a particular road about which he would say 'this is it' — and it is the way of HaShem in Torah and commandments. For he had already said ״אָכֵן יֵשׁ ה׳ בַּמָּקוֹם הַזֶּה״ (Genesis 28:16), for from there Torah would go forth; therefore he said ״הַזֶּה״ as if pointing with his finger at something that is before him — and it is the House of Elohim, from which a paved path is trodden to walk in the ways of HaShem, blessed be He. And because walking in the way of the Torah depends on a person's free choice, therefore he added the word ״אָנֹכִי״ and said ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי הוֹלֵךְ״ — 'upon me does the matter depend,' together with the divine assistance concerning which he said ״וּשְׁמָרַנִי״. And therefore he said ״וְשַׁבְתִּי בְשָׁלוֹם״ — shalem (whole) from sin — and he suspended the return upon himself, for the matter depends on his free choice, as stated. And regarding the matter of the guarding of the body, He said ״וַהֲשִׁבֹתִיךָ אֶל הָאֲדָמָה״, for the eyes of HaShem seek out the land of Israel continually (Deuteronomy 11:12); therefore it is especially in the land of Israel that one needs guarding outside the land more than in the land — consequently, no promise was needed other than that He would return him to the land, to the land of Israel, and once he arrives at the land, behold he is guarded on his own. But regarding the matter of guarding from sin — that he not learn from the ways of Laban; and not specifically Laban, for even the Canaanite dwelling in the land did not do good with his people, and there is reason to fear he might also learn from the ways of the Amorite — therefore he said ״אֶל בֵּית אָבִי״, for then he would be saved from this concern, that he not learn from the ways of the wicked." "And he inserted between the clauses a condition: ״וְנָתַן לִי לֶחֶם לֶאֱכוֹל וּבֶגֶד לִלְבּוֹשׁ״ — for the guarding from sin means that he not learn from the ways of the wicked. And there is yet another path by which every dispute and every affliction will come, and that is wealth, which blinds the eyes of its owners and prevents a person from the straight path, for it was not for nothing that Solomon sought and said (Proverbs 30:8) ״רֵשׁ וָעוֹשֶׁר אַל תִּתֶּן לִי, הַטְרִיפֵנִי לֶחֶם חֻקִּי״. Therefore Jacob also said that HaShem should withhold from him gold and an abundance of pearls, but should give him bread to eat and a garment to wear — that is, the necessary minimum, and not give him luxuries. And through this is resolved what he mentioned ״לֶאֱכוֹל״ and ״לִלְבּוֹשׁ״, for he should have said ״וְנָתַן לִי לֶחֶם וּבֶגֶד״ — and does it enter your mind that he would wear the bread and eat the garment, so that it was necessary to specify ״לֶחֶם לֶאֱכוֹל״? Rather, surely he wanted by this to exclude — that He not give him bread beyond what he eats, nor a garment beyond his need, and he would be saved from sin. Therefore he said immediately ״וְשַׁבְתִּי בְשָׁלוֹם אֶל בֵּית אָבִי״, for on this condition I will be able to return in peace — shalem (whole) from sin — as Rashi explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:06.399655880Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}